<p>Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi urged citizens and businesses on Sunday to cut petrol and diesel use, revive work-from-home practices and limit non-essential foreign travel and gold purchases as the Iran war and disruptions around the Strait of Hormuz push up energy costs and pressure India’s foreign exchange reserves. Speaking in Hyderabad, he asked people to use metro systems where available, carpool when using private vehicles, and move meetings online where possible. India has not raised petrol and diesel prices for domestic consumers or rationed supplies, but it has increased liquefied petroleum gas prices after the supply disruptions, Dawn and Business Recorder reported.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/L79ArwYbz63z6_1dzEH-4TczVtEns2_mEvlcEiDdR3brh9tQzfpki29YdyoE8npP_-OxuOvSfQUq3YY_P5cGeDMiu-xgyVC8rsVWVM_jK1Ii4hd1WEEPVgFVlJ9Uw1PCj4dofYgJ9bWjFQDocEVamO8UQV5a5wCqP4eNWX1MDgdi5FOeEPI7065DRHN4hzEUxoY' alt='Prime Minister Narendra Modi in Hyderabad, where he urged restraint in fuel use during the West Asia crisis.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Import exposure: India imports 88% of its crude oil needs and about half its natural gas requirement, much of it through the Strait of Hormuz.</li><li>Household imports: Modi also urged reduced edible oil consumption and a preference for Indian-made products as “nationally responsible” choices.</li><li>Farm inputs: He encouraged farmers to cut chemical fertilizer use by as much as 50% and move toward natural farming.</li></ul><blockquote>We must also place a strong emphasis on saving foreign exchange, as petrol and diesel have become so expensive globally. - Narendra Modi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Narendra Modi: Modi said cutting fuel use and other imports was a patriotic, “nation first” duty and that everyday choices could help India manage supply-chain and price shocks. (<a href='https://www.timesnownews.com/india/use-metro-skip-gold-avoid-foreign-travel-do-work-from-home-pm-modis-5-big-ap